{t:"Harry Potter AI Diary",s:"done",pt:"prompts/harry-potter-diary-prompt.md",d:"A physical Tom Riddle diary. V1: an ESP32-C3 makes its own WiFi AP, a captive portal opens a parchment page on any phone, and the reply types itself onto an OLED. V2 removes the phone and the buzzer: a 2.13″ e-paper book you write on with a stylus, where the answer stays on the page with the power off. Full build analysis of the firmware, the three-pass ink engine and the e-paper redesign, plus a byte-identical mirror.",how:"Constraint first: “book only” killed the phone, so the redesign started from the input device, not the display. Read the 579-line firmware line by line, enumerated the captive-portal routes from the route table, and analysed the browser ink engine from its three stroke passes before writing a word of the analysis. The mirror was copied and hash-compared rather than re-exported.",an:"WHAT IT IS\n\nA Tom Riddle diary you can hold: you write on the page, something writes back. V1 is an ESP32-C3 SuperMini with an SSD1306-family OLED that raises its own access point, forces a parchment page open on any phone through a captive portal, and types the reply onto both the page and the OLED. V2 removes the phone entirely — a LilyGO T5 V2.4 board with a 2.13″ 122×250 e-paper panel, where the answer stays on the page with the power off.\n\nTHE CONSTRAINT THAT DELETED DESIGN #1\n\n“We're not using phones, we're using the book itself only.” A diary that needs a phone is a website with a costume on. That one line invalidated the architecture, not just the parts list — no portal, no typing, no settings page, and a new hard problem: the input device.\n\nA CAPTIVE PORTAL IS SEVEN SHIMS\n\nNine routes exist so no operating system wins the argument: /generate_204 (Android), /hotspot-detect.html and /library/test/success.html (iOS), /connecttest.txt and /ncsi.txt (Windows), /canonical.html (Firefox), /redirect (Microsoft), a wildcard DNS server answering everything with 192.168.4.1, and onNotFound 302ing the rest. Remove one and the portal fails on exactly one platform — the worst kind of bug, because it works on your own phone.\n\nFIRMWARE ANATOMY\n\n579 lines, 1,026,592-byte firmware.bin, whole UI in PROGMEM as a raw-literal block so there is one thing to flash. loop() pumps DNS, pumps HTTP and animates the thinking dots every 300 ms; the JSON documents are statically sized (2048 request / 1024 response) so a week of uptime cannot fragment the heap. Text wraps at 21 columns × 6 lines with a … overflow, and the 200-token / 300-character reply cap is the size of the page, not a style choice. The API key never lives in firmware: it arrives over /settings and stays in RAM.\n\nTHE INK ENGINE\n\nThree passes per stroke: a bleed pass 2.5 px wider at alpha 0.12, a body pass whose colour jitters per segment, and a centre pass at 0.3× width that makes ink look pressed rather than painted. Width runs from 1.2 px (fast) to 3.5 px (slow), smoothed with quadratic curves through point midpoints; DPR-scaled canvas, pointer capture, leaf cleared after each answer.\n\nTHE HONEST LIMIT\n\nThe canvas is exported and thrown away — the pixels only answer “has the user written anything?”. The model never sees your handwriting; drawing alone sends a fixed “illegible handwriting” instruction. The handwriting is theatre, not OCR, and the page says so.\n\nV2 — THE PHYSICS OF PAPER\n\nT5 V2.4: ESP32 LX6 240 MHz, 4 MB flash, 2.13″ 122×250 SPI panel, ~2 s full refresh, ~300 µA idle, panel power gated by GPIO12. Three consequences: 2 s latency is terrible for a UI and perfect for a Horcrux (the page blanks, then the answer is simply there); partial refresh leaves ghosting, so the firmware must alternate with full refreshes; and 122 px at ~130 dpi makes a 1.5 mm nib 8 pixels wide — the panel is the answer surface, not a writing surface. Power: 300 µA on 500 mAh ≈ 69 days idle; an exchange costs ~0.2 mAh (WiFi TX ~200 mA for ~3 s), so the idle floor dominates and deep sleep matters more than the radio.\n\nTHE INPUT PROBLEM\n\nThe T5 has no touch. A 2.13″ 4-wire resistive overlay plus XPT2046 costs ¥11–17 and four GPIOs and never registers a palm; three buttons cost nothing and remove the magic. The 14-pin “2.4″ SPI touch” module is a trap — that pinout is usually ILI9341 TFT + XPT2046, i.e. a backlit LCD, not a paper page. Check the listing says 水墨屏.\n\nCUT ON PURPOSE\n\nThe buzzer went first (a silent book is creepier, and it removed a part, a pin and a state). The phone went second, and with it most of the cleverness in V1 and none of the product.\n\nVERIFICATION\n\nV1 compiles and was flashed (1,026,592 B); portal verified against iOS and Android probe paths; no-key and bad-key error text verified. Live: analysis page, byte-identical mirror (sha256 b04e2f6a…9839b8, 20,363 B, served copy is 757 B longer, all of it injected skin + analytics tags), canned demo, every card action — checked in headless Chromium, zero console errors. NOT done: the V2 e-paper port has never been flashed.",src:"Firmware read line by line (src/main.cpp, 579 lines) and compiled (firmware.bin 1,026,592 B); captive-portal routes enumerated from the route table; ink engine analysed from its three stroke passes in ~/Desktop/harry-potter-diary.html; LilyGO T5 V2.4 figures from the LilyGO wiki (122×250 panel, ~2 s full refresh, ~300 µA, GPIO12 panel power); V2 prices are Pinduoduo estimates, V1 prices are what was spent.",g:[{c:"ac",l:"ESP32"},{l:"E-paper"},{l:"AI"},{c:"ac",l:"Ink engine"},{l:"Harry Potter"}],a:[{c:"gr",l:"Build log",o:1,u:"/builds/harry-potter-diary/"},{c:"ac",l:"Read the analysis",o:7,u:"./demos/harry-potter-diary-analysis.html"},{l:"Full mirror",o:7,u:"./demos/harry-potter-diary-mirror/"},{l:"Canned demo",o:7,u:"./demos/harry-potter-diary.html"},{l:"Build notes",o:6,p:"./prompts/harry-potter-diary-prompt.md"}],v:[{n:"V1",i:"ESP32-C3 + OLED + captive portal. Compiles and flashed — firmware.bin 1,026,592 B."},{n:"V2",L:1,i:"LilyGO T5 V2.4 2.13″ e-paper port in progress: GxEPD2, partial refresh, stylus overlay, no phone, no buzzer."}]}
